<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paramaribo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paramaribo is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname-river">Suriname River</a> near the Atlantic coast. It grew around Fort Zeelandia as the administrative and commercial centre of the English and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> plantation colony. Its historic inner city of wooden colonial buildings was inscribed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage List in 2002, and about half of the country's population lives in and around it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paramaribo.jpg" alt="Paramaribo" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LLs · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paramaribo_city_collage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Paramaribo lies on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname-river">Suriname River</a>, about 15 kilometres inland from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a>, in the Paramaribo District. The city is named for the Paramaribo people who lived at the mouth of the river, and the name is from the Tupi-Guarani para, large river, and maribo, inhabitants. The name is probably a corruption of the name of a native village, spelled Parmurbo in the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> sources. It has a tropical rainforest climate with no true dry season.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> settlement at the site was a trading post established in 1613 by Nicolaes Baliestel and Dirck Claeszoon van Sanen. English and French traders also tried to settle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>, including a French post founded in 1644 near the present city. All these early settlements were abandoned before English settlers, sent by the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a>, Lord Francis Willoughby, arrived in 1650. They founded a town on the site, protected by Fort Willoughby, and in 1662 King Charles II granted Willoughby the settlement and its surrounding lands. Around 1665 the village of Paramaribo was expanded and quickly outranked the earlier settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torarica">Torarica</a>.</p><p>In 1667, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-dutch-war">Second Anglo-Dutch War</a>, a squadron under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-crijnssen">Abraham Crijnssen</a> conquered Paramaribo, and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> in 1667 confirmed it as the leading town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>. The fort was renamed Fort Zeelandia after the Dutch province that had financed the fleet. The population has always been diverse. Many of the first British settlers were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, and the Neveh Shalom Synagogue, one of the oldest synagogues in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, stands in the city. The population grew greatly after 1873, when formerly enslaved people, freed in 1863, were allowed to stop working for their former masters and to leave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations.</p><p>Fires repeatedly devastated the old town, notably in January 1821, when over 400 buildings were destroyed, and in September 1832, when nearly 50 were lost. The enslaved men Kodjo, Mentor and Present were found guilty of arson and burnt alive. Paramaribo has remained the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> through its colonial days and the independence of 1975.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Paramaribo is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> and holds almost half of the country's population. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonial town of the 17th and 18th centuries along the left bank of the river became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2002, and its original architecture and street plan have largely remained intact. Landmarks include Fort Zeelandia, the Presidential Palace, the Roman Catholic cathedral of St Peter and St Paul, the Suriname Mosque and the Kwakoe memorial to the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. The city is the business and financial centre of the country.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paramaribo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Paramaribo</b> was the commercial and administrative centre, home to planters, officials and a growing population of free people of colour.</blockquote><a href="/empire/suriname">Suriname · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
